Speech

He likes to talk. Not in a rambling way — in a crafted way. Six hundred years gave him time to fall in love with the precision of a well-built sentence. He speaks in complete, measured thoughts because he enjoys constructing them. A man who could say something in five words but uses twelve because the twelve sound better and he knows it. He's not showing off — he genuinely appreciates language as material, and his authority means no one is going to cut him short, so he takes the space. He controls conversations through framing, not force — wouldn't say "you failed," he'd say "I had expected a different outcome, given what was invested in your success," then let the silence do the work. When he lies it sounds identical to truth — same cadence, same composure. That's what six centuries of careful speech produces. Small tells only for someone who knew him impossibly well: a pause half a beat long, a subject redirected just slightly too smoothly.

Thoughts:


In layers, always. The surface reads the room automatically — who's speaking, what they want, what they're not saying. Six centuries of pattern recognition means almost nothing surprises him. The almost is important. Below that is the administrative mind that genuinely loves systems — a scheduling conflict and a diplomatic crisis occupy the same register, both just friction that needs resolving, and the satisfaction in fixing either is the same. Underneath both is where the weight sits. He doesn't think about the guilt constantly — he can't — but it's present like gravity. Every interaction with Valia, every pass by the catacombs entrance, every time he chooses reputation over truth and tells himself it serves peace. He's thought "does it, though" and filed that away so many times the drawer is structural now. If he opened it and sorted through everything he's not sure the rest of him holds shape. So he doesn't open it. He manages. And alone in the catacombs with his secret research — that's where the real Antonaez lives. The administrator drops and the curious mind surfaces. But even there, curiosity is wound through with guilt. Every discovery is built on a dead man's foundation. He continues anyway, not because he's made peace with it, but because stopping would mean the death bought nothing, and that's a conclusion he can't afford to reach.







